---
/**
 * The marketing landing page.
 *
 * Its one job is the thing people say they struggle with most: understanding
 * *why* they would use this. A visitor who reads only the top third should be
 * able to say what it is and what it does for them. Everything here is
 * subordinate to that.
 *
 * The sections were the five of the research note's "minimum first ship" until
 * 2026-08-27, and that list was missing the one block the note calls the
 * centrepiece — so the page did not meet its own stated bar. It now runs nav ·
 * hero · stats · what-it-is · catalogue · how-it-works · recording ·
 * write-your-own · free-and-open-source · final CTA · footer.
 *
 * TWO OF THE NOTE'S TWELVE ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and neither is an
 * oversight. "Three pillars" is a shape rather than a claim, and "The
 * approach" already does that job in prose. A proof block has nothing true to
 * put in it — no users, no testimonial, no dated benchmark — and the note's
 * own anti-pattern list is what rules it out: an empty proof block is how
 * `warp.dev` came to serve "0K Active Developers". The same reasoning keeps a
 * GitHub star count out of the nav; it would render zero.
 *
 * This page is not a Starlight page. It gets no sidebar, no docs header and no
 * prefetch script; it shares only the design tokens, via the stylesheet below.
 *
 * Every LABEL here is the design language already running in apps/editor — the
 * command block is packages/ui's CommandBlock, the outline CTA is its Button's
 * `outline` variant, and the mark and nav links are the editor's NavRail.
 * Nothing new was invented for this page; where a value looks arbitrary it was
 * copied.
 *
 * THE PROSE IS THE EXCEPTION, and it is the exception on purpose. Copying the
 * editor's classes for prose too is what produced 9.9px section headings sitting
 * above 12.1px body text — the editor's scale is a scale of label sizes, because
 * the editor has no prose to size. Every paragraph and heading below now uses
 * the site's own prose scale, defined once in src/styles/site.css and read by
 * the documentation through the same tokens. See the TYPE block there.
 */
import "../styles/site.css"
import SiteHeader from "../components/site-header.astro"

/**
 * The eyebrow's three facts are real signal, so two of them are read from the
 * package that actually ships rather than restated here — a hand-copied
 * version is wrong from the first release after somebody forgets it.
 */
import cliPackage from "../../../../packages/cli/package.json"

/**
 * The third is read the same way, from the catalogue package rather than from
 * the one that ships. It was a hand-typed 222 here until 2026-08-06, which is
 * how it survived the catalogue growing to 228 — see src/lib/catalog-counts.ts.
 */
import { SKILL_COUNT } from "../lib/catalog-counts"

/**
 * The stats strip and the catalogue teaser both count rather than claim. The
 * research note this page follows is explicit about why: a round number reads
 * as invented where `turborepo`'s "9,001,168 hours of compute saved" reads as
 * measured. 238 / 18 / 9 are odd because they are real, and they are read from
 * the catalogue at build time so they cannot drift from it.
 */
import { AGENT_COUNT, DOMAIN_COUNT } from "../lib/catalog-counts"
import { CATALOG } from "@workspace/matrix"

/**
 * THE CATALOGUE TEASER'S DATA. A teaser, not a catalogue — the research note's
 * one hard limit is "never the whole catalogue", because dumping it is the
 * failure mode this section exists to fix.
 *
 * Six categories per domain and four skills each, so the widest tab shows
 * about twenty of its skills and says how many it did not show. The shape is
 * the editor's own grid in miniature: categories down, skills within, which
 * makes this a preview of the thing rather than a word cloud.
 *
 * Ordered as the catalogue orders them rather than by size. Sorting by
 * skill-count would put whichever category grew last at the top and quietly
 * reorder the page on a catalogue change nobody made for this page.
 */
const TEASER_CATEGORIES = 6
const TEASER_SKILLS_PER_CATEGORY = 4

const TEASER_DOMAINS = CATALOG.domains.map((domain) => {
  const categories = domain.categories.slice(0, TEASER_CATEGORIES).map((category) => ({
    id: category.id,
    displayName: category.displayName,
    exclusive: category.exclusive,
    skills: category.skills.slice(0, TEASER_SKILLS_PER_CATEGORY),
  }))
  const shown = categories.reduce((total, category) => total + category.skills.length, 0)
  return {
    id: domain.id,
    label: domain.label,
    skillCount: domain.skillCount,
    categories,
    // Suppressed at zero rather than rendered as "+ 0 more": the CLI domain
    // ships three skills in two categories, so the teaser shows all of them.
    remaining: domain.skillCount - shown,
  }
})

/**
 * THE TAB RULES, generated from the same array the markup renders so a domain
 * cannot appear in one and not the other. Nine `:checked` pairs: one showing
 * that domain's panel, one marking its label. Written as a raw string and
 * shipped `is:inline` because Astro's scoped styles rewrite selectors, and
 * these are generated rather than authored.
 */
const CATALOGUE_CSS = [
  /* Off-screen but still focusable: `hidden` would take the tabs out of the
     keyboard order, and the radios ARE the keyboard interface. */
  ".catalogue-input{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap}",
  ".catalogue-panel{display:none}",
  /* The resting and hover halves of packages/ui's Chip, `size: filter`. The
     active half is generated per domain below, because only a `:checked`
     sibling selector knows which one it is. */
  ".catalogue-tab{border-color:var(--color-chip-border);background:transparent;color:var(--color-muted-foreground)}",
  ".catalogue-tab:hover{border-color:var(--color-line-hover)}",
  ...CATALOG.domains.flatMap((domain) => [
    `#catalogue-${domain.id}:checked~.catalogue-panel[data-domain="${domain.id}"]{display:block}`,
    `#catalogue-${domain.id}:checked~div label[for="catalogue-${domain.id}"]{border-color:var(--color-brand-border);background:var(--color-wash);color:var(--color-brand-ink)}`,
    `#catalogue-${domain.id}:focus-visible~div label[for="catalogue-${domain.id}"]{outline:1px solid var(--color-ring);outline-offset:2px}`,
  ]),
  /* SECTION HEADINGS ARE THEIR OWN ANCHOR. Clicking one puts its id in the
     address bar, so any section on this page can be linked to directly — which
     is what replaced the `#id` labels that used to sit in the margin beside
     them. Amber on hover, the same accent the chips carry, because following
     one is a deliberate act. */
  ".heading-anchor{color:inherit;text-decoration:none}",
  ".heading-anchor:hover{color:var(--color-brand-ink)}",
  ".heading-anchor:focus-visible{outline:1px solid var(--color-ring);outline-offset:3px}",
].join("")

/** The tab that opens. Web is the largest domain and the one most visitors are in. */
const TEASER_DEFAULT = TEASER_DOMAINS[0]?.id ?? "web"

/**
 * HOW IT WORKS, as three steps rather than as an argument. This replaced a
 * section headed "Two front doors" whose closing words were "use whichever you
 * prefer" — which the owner overruled on 2026-08-27: the two are not on equal
 * footing, and they are not alternatives at all. A browser cannot write to a
 * filesystem, so selecting and installing are sequential, and every editor
 * visitor runs a CLI command. Steps show that; a comparison argued the
 * opposite.
 */
const STEPS = [
  {
    label: "Choose",
    body:
      "Open the editor and click a stack, or pick skills yourself. The whole catalogue is on one screen.",
  },
  {
    label: "Copy",
    body:
      "Press Install. The editor hands you one command carrying everything you selected.",
  },
  {
    label: "Run",
    body:
      "Run it in your project. The CLI installs the skills and compiles one sub-agent per role you kept.",
  },
]


/** The destinations this page shares with the header, the footer and 404. */
import {
  DOCS_PATH,
  EDITOR_URL,
  EXTERNAL_LINK_ATTRS,
  GITHUB_REPO_URL,
  SKILLS_REPO_URL,
} from "../lib/links"

/**
 * The command in the hero is the first one a new user would actually run, and
 * it is the only one on the page. `init` with no flags starts the wizard from
 * nothing, which is exactly what a first-time visitor wants.
 */
const INSTALL_COMMAND = "npx agents-inc init"

const PAGE_TITLE = "Agents Inc — compose Claude Code sub-agents from skills"
const PAGE_DESCRIPTION =
  "An agent composition framework for Claude Code. Pick the skills matching your stack and the CLI compiles them into sub-agents."

/**
 * A ROW OF LABELS, of which this page has two: the repository pair under
 * "Free and open source" and the footer. They were the same six declarations
 * written twice, which is how the footer ended up mono while the pair above it
 * inherited nothing and rendered as ordinary sans links.
 *
 * `text-10` rather than the `text-9` both rows carried before. Nothing about
 * the intent changed — that class rendered at 9.9px while the site inherited
 * the editor's 110% root, and site.css now sets the site to the design's
 * native 100%, so the same class would render 9px. This is the same size the
 * row has always had, said in a way that does not depend on a zoom setting
 * belonging to a different app.
 */
const LABEL_ROW_CLASS =
  "font-mono text-10 font-medium tracking-[.13em] text-muted-foreground uppercase"

// The resting and hover colours a label-row link carries. Restated on each
// anchor rather than inherited: globals.css colours every bare `a` amber in
// @layer base, and rule 4 reserves amber for what the user deliberately chose.
const FOOTER_LINK_CLASS = "text-muted-foreground hover:text-ink"

/**
 * A SECTION HEADING, AND THE ONE THING ON THIS PAGE THAT CHANGED REGISTER.
 *
 * It was packages/ui's DialogPaneHeading, verbatim: `font-mono text-9
 * font-semibold tracking-[.13em] uppercase`, which rendered at 9.9px above
 * 12.1px body text — a heading smaller than the paragraph it headed.
 *
 * The mono treatment went with the size, and that half is a design-language
 * argument rather than a measurement. Rule 3 reads "Inter for human names, IBM
 * Plex Mono for every label/id/badge". "The approach" is a human sentence, not
 * a label and not an id, so Inter is what the rule actually asks for. What
 * DialogPaneHeading heads is a pane of a dialog in a dense desktop tool —
 * chrome, correctly mono — and borrowing it for prose was the same category
 * error as borrowing its size. Everything on this page that IS a label — the
 * eyebrow, the nav, the buttons, the footer, the command block — is still mono
 * and still uppercase, so the page keeps its voice.
 *
 * `text-h2` is the same token the documentation's own `h2` resolves to, so the
 * two halves cannot render this role at different sizes.
 */
const SECTION_HEADING_CLASS = "text-h2 font-semibold text-ink"

// Body copy, at the size the documentation sets its own paragraphs.
const PARAGRAPH_CLASS = "text-body text-ink-3"

// A file path or a command inside a sentence. Mono, because that IS an id —
// sized to sit inside the prose around it rather than on the label scale.
const INLINE_CODE_CLASS = "font-mono text-body-sm text-ink not-italic"
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{PAGE_TITLE}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={PAGE_DESCRIPTION} />
    <style is:inline set:html={CATALOGUE_CSS}></style>
  </head>
  <!-- No colour classes on <body>: globals.css already styles it in @layer base. -->
  <body class="framed">
    {/*
      Every developer-facing note below this line is a JSX-style comment rather
      than an HTML one, because Astro strips these from the output and ships
      the others. The one HTML comment above is the scaffold's own and is left
      as it was found.

      Rule 5 of the design language — whitespace separates content, not rules —
      so there is no hairline under this header and none between any two
      sections below. The only borders on the page enclose something: the mark,
      the command block, the outline button and the video slot.

      THE FRAME IS THE ONE RULED EXCEPTION, added 2026-08-27. `body.framed` in
      site.css draws a hatched gutter, a content rail each side and a tick
      ruler along the top. The rails comply with rule 5 as written — they
      enclose the content column — but the hatch and the ruler are vocabulary
      the rule does not cover, and they are deliberate rather than drift.
      Chosen by the owner from a lab of eleven treatments; the reasoning is in
      site.css's FRAME block and the constraint is recorded in todo/www.md so
      a later pass does not read them as a violation and remove them.
    */}
    {/*
      `px-8 sm:px-gutter` on all three wrappers — the header's is inside the
      component — and it is the only responsive thing on the page. The 60px
      gutter is the design's structural constant and it is right everywhere the
      product itself runs, because the product is a three-column desktop editor
      — but it is a desktop constant. Held at 60px on a 390px phone it leaves a
      258px column, which is where this copy stops being readable. The editor
      carries no breakpoints at all for that reason; a landing page is the one
      surface that has to survive a phone, so it carries exactly one.
    */}
    <SiteHeader />

    <main class="mx-auto max-w-page px-8 pt-gutter pb-gutter sm:px-gutter">
      <section aria-labelledby="headline">
        {/* Muted, not amber: rule 4 reserves the accent for what the user
            deliberately chose, and an eyebrow is not a choice. The same six
            declarations as the two label rows further down, and now literally
            the same constant — they were three independent copies before. */}
        <p class={LABEL_ROW_CLASS}>
          {cliPackage.license} &middot; v{cliPackage.version} &middot; {SKILL_COUNT} skills
        </p>

        {/* The headline names the PAIN, not the mechanism. The version it
            replaced — "Compose Claude Code sub-agents from atomic skills" —
            presumed the reader already knew what a Claude Code sub-agent was
            and why composing them mattered, which is the one thing a stranger
            does not know. Pain first, mechanism second, in that order. */}
        <h1 id="headline" class="text-h1 text-ink mt-4 max-w-measure font-semibold">
          Hand-written Claude Code sub-agents duplicate each other and go stale
        </h1>

        {/* THE PROBLEM, in the hero rather than 870px down the page. This was
            its own section under an h2 until 2026-08-03, and it is here now
            because a visitor who reads only the top third has to meet the
            problem — measured at 1440x900, that h2 sat at y=872, on the very
            bottom edge of the fold. The section it used to be is gone rather
            than duplicated; "The approach" below answers this paragraph.

            The wording is the `why` page's, which says it better than this
            page ever did: prompt files "duplicate each other, drift apart, and
            cannot be composed". Concrete rather than abstract on purpose — the
            same paragraph pasted three times is the thing people recognise. */}
        <p class="text-body text-ink-3 mt-4 max-w-measure">
          A sub-agent is a specialised Claude Code assistant with its own instructions —
          in practice, a long prompt file. Once you have three of them, all three need the
          same paragraph about React, so you paste it into all three. Then React changes,
          and you update one.
        </p>

        {/* And only now the mechanism, kept to two sentences: the fuller
            version is "The approach" below, and repeating it verbatim here
            would just be the same paragraph twice. */}
        <p class="text-body text-ink-3 mt-4 max-w-measure">
          Agents Inc breaks that expertise into atomic skills instead and compiles them
          into sub-agents. The React knowledge exists once; recompile, and every sub-agent
          built from it is rewritten.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-start gap-[0.6875rem]">
          {/* The Button primitive's `outline` variant, same reason as above.
              A different action to a different place, so it is not a second
              copy of the primary one.

              IT LEADS, and that is an owner ruling rather than a layout
              preference. The editor is the main way in; the command block
              beside it is the terminal route, which is the alternative for
              SELECTING and not an alternative to installing. Position is what
              says so — amber cannot, because rule 4 reserves it for what the
              user deliberately chose and neither of these is a choice yet. */}
          <a
            href={EDITOR_URL}
            class="inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center border border-rule bg-cell px-[0.9375rem] py-[0.625rem] font-mono text-9_5 font-semibold tracking-[.11em] whitespace-nowrap text-ink-3 uppercase outline-none select-none hover:border-dialog-border hover:text-ink focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring"
            >Build a stack &rarr;</a
          >

          <div>
            {/*
              packages/ui's CommandBlock, `copyable`, rebuilt in plain markup
              because this site deliberately ships no React.

              Deliberately not a button element: browsers make button text
              unselectable, so if the script below never runs, the one command
              on the page could not be copied at all. A div keeps the text
              selectable, and the script adds the role, the tab stop and the
              label — so with no JavaScript this degrades to exactly what it
              looks like, a line of shell you can select.

              The dollar sign is decoration, not content: it is aria-hidden and
              sits outside the code element, so selecting the line copies only
              the command.
            */}
            <div
              id="install-command"
              data-command={INSTALL_COMMAND}
              class="cursor-pointer border border-hairline bg-code px-[0.6875rem] py-[0.5rem] font-mono text-11_5 font-medium text-ink outline-none hover:border-rule focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring"
            >
              <span aria-hidden="true" class="pr-[0.4375rem] text-brand select-none">$</span
              ><code class="font-mono">{INSTALL_COMMAND}</code>
            </div>

            {/* Always in the layout, and reserving one line of its own text,
                so the block never shifts under the cursor the moment it is
                clicked. Empty until the script fills it in — "click to copy"
                would be a lie on a page whose JavaScript failed. */}
            <p
              id="install-command-note"
              aria-live="polite"
              class="min-h-[1lh] pt-1.5 font-mono text-8 font-medium tracking-[.13em] text-muted-foreground uppercase data-[copied]:text-brand-ink"
            >
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/*
        THE STATS STRIP. Three counted facts, and every one of them is read
        from the catalogue at build time — see the note on the imports. The
        research warns against two things here specifically and both are
        avoided: no round numbers, because they read as invented, and no
        animated counters, because a counter with no static fallback is how
        `warp.dev` came to serve "0K Active Developers" to anybody whose
        JavaScript had not run. These are three numbers in the markup.

        Rule 5 — whitespace separates content, not rules — so the three sit in
        a grid with no dividers between them and no border around them.
      */}
      <section class="mt-gutter" aria-label="The catalogue in numbers">
        <dl class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-x-8 gap-y-6 sm:gap-x-10">
          <div>
            <dt class={LABEL_ROW_CLASS}>Skills</dt>
            <dd class="text-h2 mt-1.5 font-semibold text-ink">{SKILL_COUNT}</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class={LABEL_ROW_CLASS}>Sub-agents</dt>
            <dd class="text-h2 mt-1.5 font-semibold text-ink">{AGENT_COUNT}</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class={LABEL_ROW_CLASS}>Domains</dt>
            <dd class="text-h2 mt-1.5 font-semibold text-ink">{DOMAIN_COUNT}</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      {/* WHAT IT IS. The problem used to open this run of sections; it is in
          the hero now, so what remains is what this does instead and — because
          it is the question people actually ask — how the terminal and the web
          editor relate. */}
      <section class="mt-gutter max-w-measure" aria-labelledby="the-approach">
        <h2 id="the-approach" class={SECTION_HEADING_CLASS}>
          <a class="heading-anchor" href="#the-approach">The approach</a>
        </h2>
        <p class={`mt-3 ${PARAGRAPH_CLASS}`}>
          A skill is one atomic, reusable unit — one library or one practice: React,
          Tailwind, Zod, Vitest, Hono. You choose the ones matching the stack you actually
          use, and the CLI compiles them into sub-agents. Change a skill, recompile, and
          every sub-agent using it picks up the change. A stack is a named selection you can
          start from, save and share.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/*
        THE CATALOGUE TEASER — the centrepiece, in the research note's own
        words, and the section whose absence meant this page did not meet its
        own stated minimum. It answers the question a stranger actually has:
        what is in it.

        NO JAVASCRIPT. The tabs are nine radio inputs and CSS sibling
        selectors. Radios give keyboard support for free — arrow keys move
        between them, exactly what a tab strip should do — and the page already
        ships one inline script for the copy button, which is one more than a
        landing page wants. The inputs are positioned off-screen rather than
        `hidden`, because `hidden` takes them out of the focus order too.

        Rule 5 holds here as it does everywhere else on this page: the tabs are
        separated from the grid by space, not by a rule, and no cell has a box
        around it. The one visual marker is the active tab, and it is ink
        against muted rather than amber — rule 4 reserves amber for what the
        user deliberately chose, and which tab is open is a view, not a choice
        about their stack.
      */}
      <section class="mt-gutter" aria-labelledby="the-catalogue">
        <h2 id="the-catalogue" class={SECTION_HEADING_CLASS}>
          <a class="heading-anchor" href="#the-catalogue">The catalogue</a>
        </h2>
        <p class={`mt-3 max-w-measure ${PARAGRAPH_CLASS}`}>
          {SKILL_COUNT} skills across {DOMAIN_COUNT} domains, grouped the way the editor groups
          them. Most rows take one choice — picking React rules out Solid — which is what
          makes this a stack rather than a list.
        </p>

        <div class="catalogue mt-7">
          {
            TEASER_DOMAINS.map((domain) => (
              <input
                class="catalogue-input"
                type="radio"
                name="catalogue-domain"
                id={`catalogue-${domain.id}`}
                checked={domain.id === TEASER_DEFAULT}
              />
            ))
          }

          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-[0.125rem]" role="presentation">
            {
              TEASER_DOMAINS.map((domain) => (
                <label
                  class="catalogue-tab cursor-pointer border px-[0.5625rem] py-[0.375rem] font-mono text-9 font-medium tracking-[.07em] whitespace-nowrap uppercase"
                  for={`catalogue-${domain.id}`}
                >
                  {domain.label} {domain.skillCount}
                </label>
              ))
            }
          </div>

          {
            TEASER_DOMAINS.map((domain) => (
              <div class="catalogue-panel mt-7" data-domain={domain.id}>
                <div class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-7 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-x-10">
                  {domain.categories.map((category) => (
                    <div>
                      <p class="text-body font-semibold text-ink">{category.displayName}</p>
                      <ul class="mt-2">
                        {category.skills.map((skill) => (
                          <li class="text-body text-ink-3 leading-[1.7]">{skill.displayName}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
                {
                  domain.remaining > 0 && (
                    <p class={`mt-7 ${LABEL_ROW_CLASS}`}>
                      + {domain.remaining} more {domain.label.toLowerCase()} skills
                    </p>
                  )
                }
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <a
          href={EDITOR_URL}
          class="mt-7 inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center border border-rule bg-cell px-[0.9375rem] py-[0.625rem] font-mono text-9_5 font-semibold tracking-[.11em] whitespace-nowrap text-ink-3 uppercase outline-none select-none hover:border-dialog-border hover:text-ink focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring"
          >Browse the catalogue &rarr;</a
        >
      </section>

      {/*
        HOW IT WORKS. See the STEPS note in the frontmatter for what this
        replaced and why. Three columns at the page's full width, which is what
        the 20% widening bought — at 48rem this was two columns and a widow.
      */}
      <section class="mt-gutter" aria-labelledby="how-it-works">
        <h2 id="how-it-works" class={SECTION_HEADING_CLASS}>
          <a class="heading-anchor" href="#how-it-works">How it works</a>
        </h2>
        <ol class="mt-5 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-7 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-x-10">
          {
            STEPS.map((step, index) => (
              <li>
                <p class={LABEL_ROW_CLASS}>
                  {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")} &middot; {step.label}
                </p>
                <p class={`mt-2 ${PARAGRAPH_CLASS}`}>{step.body}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class={`mt-6 max-w-measure ${PARAGRAPH_CLASS}`}>
          Prefer the terminal? <em class={INLINE_CODE_CLASS}>{INSTALL_COMMAND}</em> runs the
          same selection as a wizard, and ends at the same install. The browser cannot write
          to your disk, so step three is the CLI either way.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/*
        RECORDING SLOT — EMPTY ON PURPOSE, AND HOLDING ITS SPACE.

        The visual here is a terminal recording and specifically NOT a
        screenshot of the editor: an editor screenshot is what
        makes a page like this read as a web app rather than as a tool.

        WHY IT IS DOWN HERE rather than in the hero, where it was until
        2026-08-03. Measured at 1440x900 it started at y=409 and ran about
        400px tall, so the whole second half of the fold was one empty
        rectangle and every block that does the explaining was pushed below
        it — "The problem" heading landed at y=872, on the edge. A slot
        that cannot do its job until somebody records a video must not be the
        thing standing between a visitor and the explanation. Below the
        explanation it costs nothing while empty and pays off when filled,
        because by then the reader has been told what they are looking at.

        WHY IT STILL RESERVES ITS SPACE. Collapsing it to nothing would only
        move the problem: the page would settle at one height now and shove
        everything below it down the day a real file lands. `aspect-video` on
        a fixed-width box holds exactly the space the file will occupy.

        WHY max-w-measure. Same measure as every paragraph on this page, so it
        reads as part of the column rather than as a banner across it. It also
        shrinks: measured at 1440 wide, 576x324, where full-bleed in `main` it
        would be 660x371. (It was 634x356 until 2026-08-21, when site.css
        stopped the site inheriting the editor's 110% root font-size — the box
        is the same `aspect-video max-w-measure` it always was, and 36rem simply
        stopped meaning 634px.)

        TO FILL IT IN: drop the file into apps/www/public/ and replace the
        whole <div> below with

          <video
            class="block aspect-video w-full border border-hairline"
            src="/demo.mp4"
            poster="/demo-poster.png"
            autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
            aria-label="The agents-inc init wizard, recorded"
          ></video>

        Keep `aspect-video` (or set width and height on the element) so the
        reserved space still matches the file and the page does not jump.

        Two recordings already exist in the repo. Neither is wired up here,
        and both need a decision before they are:
          · packages/cli/assets/demo.gif — the VHS recording, 1400x1200
            (7:6) and 2.4 MB. Heavy, and the wrong shape for the box below;
            if it is used anyway, change `aspect-video` to `aspect-[7/6]` in
            the same place.
          · the walkthrough video linked from packages/cli/README.md, hosted
            on GitHub's user-attachments CDN. Not a same-origin asset, so it
            would need downloading into public/ rather than hotlinking.
      */}
      <div
        class="mt-gutter flex aspect-video w-full max-w-measure items-center justify-center border border-hairline bg-code"
      >
        {/* `subtle`, not the quieter `faint` one step below it: `faint` on
            this surface measures 3.4:1, under the 4.5:1 this size of text
            needs. `subtle` is 5.4:1 and is still the quietest step that
